-- title:
--   Triviality of J₀(p) for primes p<5
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number carrying the typeclass assumption that it is prime, and suppose $p<5$. The theorem asserts that the type `JZero p` is a subsingleton, i.e. any two of its elements are equal. Here `JZero N` is the project's degree-zero divisor class group attached to level $N$: it is `Pic0` of the field `modularFunctionFieldBar N` over the base field $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, where `modularFunctionFieldBar N` is the base change to $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ (in the sense of `laurentBaseChange`, the subfield of $\overline{\mathbf Q}$-Laurent series generated by the coefficientwise image) of the intermediate field `modularFunctionFieldFull N` of $\mathbf Q((q))$ obtained by adjoining to $\mathbf Q$ the $q$-expansions $j(q^d)$ for all divisors $d\mid N$. Concretely, `Pic0` is the quotient of the subgroup of degree-zero divisors — finitely supported $\mathbf Z$-valued functions on the places of that function field over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, with degree computed through the residue degrees — by the subgroup of principal divisors. Thus the conclusion says that, for $p$ prime and $p<5$, every degree-zero divisor class of the level-$p$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ is trivial; equivalently, the Jacobian $J_0(p)$ over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$, as modelled in this development by the divisor class group of a function field, has exactly one point. Note that the statement is about subsingletonness of a type, and carries no nontriviality or inhabitedness content beyond what `Pic0` supplies.
--
--   Classically this is the statement that the modular curves $X_0(2)$ and $X_0(3)$ have genus zero, so their Jacobians vanish; in this development genus zero is expressed through the triviality of the degree-zero divisor class group of the corresponding function field over $\overline{\mathbf Q}$ rather than through a genus computation. The formal statement is uniform in the prime $p<5$, whereas the textbook statement is usually made separately for the two curves. It is used to dispose of the small primes in the construction of good identity components of Néron models ([`ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroNeronIdentityComponentGood`](thm.html#ModularCurve.nonempty_jZeroNeronIdentityComponentGood)), in the finiteness and torsion statements for Eisenstein quotients of `heckeModuleBar`, and in the cohomological bound [`ModularCurve.jZeroNeronTorsionSheaf_h0_bounded_v5`](thm.html#ModularCurve.jZeroNeronTorsionSheaf_h0_bounded_v5), where the main arguments require $p\ge 5$.
